  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Contributing Towards the Humanitarian, Development and Peace Nexus in Mozambique</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">For more than six years, northern Mozambique has experienced attacks by non-state armed groups, resulting in large scale displacements, and consequentially a complex crisis and dire humanitarian situation. The vast majority of the internally displaced persons (IDPs) in the country (namely 545,870 out of a total of 721,348 people) are in the Northern provinces, with 55 per cent of IDPs settled within host communities. In addition, more than six hundred thousand persons have returned to their places of origin. Through this project, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) is contributing towards the provision of a comprehensive response and multisectoral assistance for displaced people, returnees and affected communities in Northern Mozambique through an area-based approach.   Integrated multisectoral support is being implemented in areas with a high concentration of needs, where humanitarian and development needs overlap, including hard-to-reach areas. This approach enhances emergency humanitarian interventions and coordination to strengthen service delivery capacities while directly reinforcing the resilience of affected communities and their living conditions. The intervention complements IOM&apos;s existing emergency response programme to ensure the provision of comprehensive support to communities impacted by displacement while enhancing the overall impact of current projects focused on providing adequate and tailored solutions.</narrative>
  </description>
